var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#NCCL.jl","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"A Julia wrapper for the NVIDIA Collective Communications Library (NCCL).","title":"NCCL.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"Buffers passed to collective and point-to-point operations must be stored contiguously in memory of the device associated with the communicator: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. Operations temporarily activate that device, and restore the previously active device when they return. They are issued on the stream keyword argument, which defaults to the task-local stream of the communicator's device (CUDACore.stream() while that device is active), and must belong to that device; the per-thread default stream (per_thread_stream()) is not supported. Arguments are validated before calling NCCL: buffers whose lengths don't fit the operation throw a DimensionMismatch, and other invalid arguments (e.g. non-contiguous buffers, mismatched element types, ranks out of range, or partially overlapping buffers) an ArgumentError.","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"#Creating-communicators-with-MPI","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"The ranks of a communicator need to agree on an NCCL.UniqueID, which one of them creates and sends to the others. In a program that uses MPI, loading MPI.jl enables NCCL.Communicator(mpicomm), which does this over an MPI communicator: rank 0 creates the identifier and broadcasts it, and every MPI rank becomes the NCCL rank with the same index.\n\nEach process has to use a different GPU of its node. Unless each process can only see a single GPU (e.g. by setting CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES per process), select one based on the rank of the process among the processes on the same node:\n\nusing MPI, CUDACore, NCCL\nMPI.Init()\n\nworld_rank = MPI.Comm_rank(MPI.COMM_WORLD)\nnode_comm = MPI.Comm_split_type(MPI.COMM_WORLD, MPI.COMM_TYPE_SHARED, world_rank)\nMPI.Comm_size(node_comm) <= ndevices() ||\n    error(\"more processes on this node than GPUs\")\nCUDACore.device!(MPI.Comm_rank(node_comm))\n\nNCCL.Communicator(MPI.COMM_WORLD) do comm\n    buf = CUDACore.fill(Float32(world_rank), 1024)\n    NCCL.Allreduce!(buf, +, comm)\nend\n\nRun it with MPI.jl's launcher, e.g. (MPImpiexec()) -n 4 julia --project scriptjl.","title":"Creating communicators with MPI"},{"category":"section","location":"#Element-types","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL natively supports buffers of Int8, UInt8, Int32, UInt32, Int64, UInt64, Float16, Float32, Float64 and BFloat16 (from BFloat16s.jl) elements, which can be used with every operation and reduction operator. Some other element types are passed to NCCL as one of these:\n\ncomplex numbers with Float16, Float32, Float64 or BFloat16 parts are passed as pairs of real numbers. NCCL then reduces the real and imaginary parts separately, which is only correct for +, NCCL.avg and NCCL.PreMulSum with a real scalar: reducing complex numbers with *, max or min throws an ArgumentError.\nBools are passed as bytes. They can only be reduced with max (logical or) and min (logical and), since sums and averages of booleans are not booleans.\nother isbits types (e.g. Int16, Char, tuples or immutable structs) are passed as bytes, and can only be copied: they are supported by data movement operations (Broadcast!, Allgather!, Alltoall!, Gather!, Scatter!, Send and Recv!), but not by reductions. Padding bytes in such values are copied as well.\n\nOther element types, i.e. types that aren't isbits (including isbits unions), and types of size zero (e.g. Nothing), are not supported.","title":"Element types"},{"category":"section","location":"#Library","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"","title":"Library"},{"category":"section","location":"#Errors","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"#Communicators","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"","title":"Communicators"},{"category":"section","location":"#Groups","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"","title":"Groups"},{"category":"section","location":"#Collective-communication","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"Reductions combine the values of all ranks with +, *, max, min, NCCL.avg, or NCCL.PreMulSum, which multiplies each rank's values by a scalar before summing them.","title":"Collective communication"},{"category":"section","location":"#Point-to-point-communication","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"","title":"Point-to-point communication"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL_jll.is_available","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.is_available() :: Bool\n\nIs the NCCL library available?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL_jll.is_available"},{"category":"method","location":"#NCCL.version-Tuple{}","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.version() :: VersionNumber\n\nGet the version of the current NCCL library.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.version"},{"category":"type","location":"#NCCL.LibNCCL.NCCLError","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCLError(code::ncclResult_t, [msg::String])\n\nAn error returned by a NCCL function. code is the result code, and msg the error message NCCL reported for it (see ncclGetLastError), or an empty string if there is none.\n\nNCCL keeps a single, process-wide last error message, which it only updates when it logs a warning and never clears. msg is therefore only set when that message changed since NCCL.jl last read it, so that an error which NCCL didn't log doesn't repeat the message of an earlier one. As a consequence, msg is empty when NCCL reports the same message twice in a row, and may describe an error raised concurrently by another thread. Set the environment variable NCCL_DEBUG=WARN to see all of NCCL's messages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.LibNCCL.NCCLError"},{"category":"type","location":"#NCCL.Communicator","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Communicator\n\nAn NCCL communicator: one rank of a group of ranks that perform collective and point-to-point operations together.\n\nCommunicators are not destroyed by the garbage collector. Destroying a communicator involves communication with the other ranks, which can block, or deadlock when the ranks don't do so at the same time, so it needs to happen at a well-defined point in the program: call NCCL.destroy (or NCCL.abort after an error) when you are done with it, or use the do-block form of the constructors. A communicator that is never destroyed keeps its resources until the process exits.\n\nAfter a communicator has been destroyed or aborted, it is closed (see isopen), and using it throws an InvalidStateException. Its NCCL.rank, NCCL.nranks and NCCL.device remain available.\n\nCommunicators are always blocking; see NCCL.Config.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Communicator"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Communicators","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Communicators(devices; config=nothing) :: Vector{Communicator}\nNCCL.Communicators(f, devices; config=nothing)\n\nCreate a communicator with one rank per device in devices, all driven from the current task. Rank i-1 uses devices[i]; the devices must be distinct. devices can contain CuDevices or device ordinals.\n\nconfig can be an NCCL.Config to configure the communicator. Without it, this uses ncclCommInitAll, otherwise one ncclCommInitRankConfig per device in a group.\n\nOperations on several of these communicators have to be issued in an NCCL.group, or the first one blocks waiting for the others. Destroy the communicators together, with NCCL.destroy(comms), or use the do-block form, which destroys them when f returns, and aborts them if it throws (see NCCL.Communicator).\n\nExamples\n\nNCCL.Communicators(CUDACore.devices()) do comms\n    NCCL.group() do\n        for (comm, buf) in zip(comms, bufs)\n            NCCL.Allreduce!(buf, +, comm)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nExternal links\n\nncclCommInitAll\nncclCommInitRankConfig\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Communicators"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.split","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.split(comm::Communicator, color, key=NCCL.rank(comm);\n           config=nothing) :: Union{Communicator,Nothing}\nNCCL.split(comms::AbstractVector{Communicator}, colors, keys=NCCL.rank.(comms);\n           config=nothing) :: Vector{Union{Communicator,Nothing}}\nNCCL.split(f, comm, color, key; config)\nNCCL.split(f, comms, colors, keys; config)\n\nSplit the communicator comm into new communicators: the ranks that pass the same color (a non-negative integer) form a new communicator, in which they are ordered by key (ranks with equal keys keep the order they have in comm). Ranks that pass nothing as color don't take part in any new communicator, and get nothing back. The new communicators use the same devices as comm.\n\nThis is a collective operation: every rank of comm has to call NCCL.split, even the ones that pass nothing, and the call blocks until all of them have. A task that drives several ranks of comm, e.g. the communicators returned by Communicators, has to split them together by passing vectors of communicators, colors and keys, which splits them in a single group; splitting them one by one deadlocks. NCCL.split cannot be called inside NCCL.group.\n\nThere must not be any outstanding operations on comm when it is split, or the split may deadlock: wait for the operations issued on comm to complete first (e.g. by synchronizing the streams they were issued on).\n\nThe new communicators use the configuration config (an NCCL.Config). Without it, they inherit the configuration of comm. If comm was created with split_share=true, the new communicators share resources with it: then comm and the new communicators must not be used concurrently, and aborting one of them can make the others unusable.\n\nThe new communicators are independent of comm, and need to be destroyed separately (see NCCL.destroy), before or after comm. With the do-block forms, they are passed to f, and destroyed when f returns, or aborted if it throws (see Communicator).\n\nIf the split fails, any new communicator that NCCL created is aborted before the error is thrown.\n\nExamples\n\n# split the ranks of `comm` in two halves\nn = NCCL.nranks(comm)\nhalf = NCCL.split(comm, NCCL.rank(comm) < n ÷ 2 ? 0 : 1)\n\n# a communicator with only the even ranks of a clique\nNCCL.Communicators(CUDACore.devices()) do comms\n    NCCL.split(comms, [isodd(NCCL.rank(c)) ? nothing : 0 for c in comms]) do evens\n        ...\n    end\nend\n\nExternal links\n\nncclCommSplit\nCreating more communicators\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.split"},{"category":"type","location":"#NCCL.UniqueID","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.UniqueID()\nNCCL.UniqueID(bytes::AbstractVector{UInt8})\n\nThe identifier that all ranks of a communicator pass to Communicator, so that they find each other.\n\nUniqueID() creates a new identifier (ncclGetUniqueId). This is done by one rank, which then has to send it to all other ranks by some other means, e.g. MPI, a socket, or a file. UniqueID is a plain bits type of 128 bytes: Vector{UInt8}(id) returns these bytes, and UniqueID(bytes) reconstructs the identifier from them. It can also be sent as-is with anything that handles bits types, e.g. Serialization or Distributed.\n\nCreating an identifier also starts a bootstrap listener on the creating process, so the rank that creates it has to take part in the communicator.\n\nExamples\n\nif rank == 0\n    id = NCCL.UniqueID()\n    write(io, Vector{UInt8}(id))\nelse\n    id = NCCL.UniqueID(read(io, 128))\nend\n\nExternal links\n\nncclGetUniqueId\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.UniqueID"},{"category":"type","location":"#NCCL.Config","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Config(; kwargs...)\n\nConfiguration for creating a communicator, passed as the config keyword argument of Communicator and Communicators. Every option defaults to nothing, which leaves the choice to NCCL (or the corresponding environment variable, which takes precedence over most options).\n\ncomm_name::String: a name for the communicator, used by NCCL in its logs and profiling.\nnet_name::String: the network module to use, e.g. \"IB\" or \"Socket\", or the name of an external network plugin (case-insensitive).\ncga_cluster_size::Integer: the cooperative group array size of the kernels NCCL launches (0 to 8).\nmin_ctas::Integer, max_ctas::Integer: the minimal and maximal number of CTAs that NCCL should use for each kernel (1 to 32).\nnvls_ctas::Integer: the total number of CTAs to use for NVLink SHARP kernels.\ncta_policy::Integer: a bitmask of NCCL.LibNCCL.NCCL_CTA_POLICY_* flags.\nsplit_share::Bool: whether communicators split from this one may share its resources.\ntraffic_class::Integer: the traffic class to use for network operations; its meaning depends on the network plugin.\ncollnet_enable::Bool: whether to use IB SHARP.\nn_channels_per_net_peer::Integer: the number of network channels used for each peer in point-to-point communication.\nmax_p2p_peers::Integer: the maximal number of peers a rank communicates with at the same time using point-to-point communication.\ngraph_usage_mode::Integer: how the communicator is used with CUDA graphs: 0 (no graphs), 1 (one graph) or 2 (multiple graphs, or graphs mixed with regular launches; the default).\n\nSee the NCCL documentation for the details of each option. Options that the NCCL library in use doesn't know about are ignored.\n\nCommunicators are always created in blocking mode: nonblocking communicators, which require polling for the completion of every NCCL call, are not supported yet. NCCL lets the environment variable NCCL_COMM_BLOCKING override this, and also reads that variable from its configuration files (the file named by NCCL_CONF_FILE, or ~/.nccl.conf, and /etc/nccl.conf). Those files only set variables that are not in the environment yet, so when NCCL.jl is loaded, or creates a communicator, while NCCL_COMM_BLOCKING is unset, it sets it to 1, which NCCL's configuration files can then not override. Processes started afterwards inherit this setting. NCCL reads the variable only once per process, so this cannot take effect if another library already initialized NCCL in the process (e.g., by creating a communicator) before NCCL.jl was loaded. If NCCL_COMM_BLOCKING is set to a value that NCCL reads as 0, creating a communicator throws an error. NCCL only parses the leading number of the value (and older versions read a value without digits as 0), so this includes values like 0x0 or 0abc.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclConfig_t\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Config"},{"category":"method","location":"#NCCL.rank-Tuple{Communicator}","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.rank(comm::Communicator) :: Int\n\nThe 0-based index of this rank in the communicator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.rank"},{"category":"method","location":"#NCCL.nranks-Tuple{Communicator}","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.nranks(comm::Communicator) :: Int\n\nThe number of ranks in the communicator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.nranks"},{"category":"method","location":"#NCCL.device-Tuple{Communicator}","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.device(comm::Communicator) :: CuDevice\n\nThe device of the communicator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.device"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.destroy","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.destroy(comm::Communicator)\nNCCL.destroy(comms::AbstractVector{Communicator})\n\nDestroy the communicator comm (or all communicators in comms), after waiting for its outstanding operations to complete. Afterwards, the communicator is closed and can no longer be used. Destroying a closed communicator does nothing.\n\nnothing, which NCCL.split returns for ranks that are not part of a new communicator, is ignored.\n\nThis first finalizes the communicator (ncclCommFinalize), which flushes its outstanding operations and synchronizes with the other ranks, and then frees its local resources (ncclCommDestroy). Finalization is an intra-node collective operation: all ranks on the same node need to destroy their communicator, or this call blocks. When a task drives several ranks of the same communicator, e.g. the communicators returned by Communicators, destroy them together by passing a vector: their finalization is then grouped, as NCCL requires. Destroying them one by one may deadlock.\n\nIf finalization fails, the communicator remains open; use NCCL.abort to free it.\n\nThis function must not be called inside NCCL.group.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclCommFinalize\nncclCommDestroy\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.destroy"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.abort","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.abort(comm::Communicator)\nNCCL.abort(comms::AbstractVector{Communicator})\n\nAbort the communicator comm (or all communicators in comms): cancel its uncompleted operations and free its resources. Afterwards, the communicator is closed and can no longer be used. Aborting a closed communicator does nothing. nothing is ignored, as with NCCL.destroy.\n\nThis is meant to release a communicator after an error. It does not wait for outstanding operations or synchronize with the other ranks, but NCCL expects every rank to abort its communicator, and peers that keep using theirs may block. Aborting a communicator is not guaranteed to be able to interrupt an operation that is blocked inside NCCL, because NCCL.jl only supports blocking communicators.\n\nThis function must not be called inside NCCL.group.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclCommAbort\nFault tolerance\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.abort"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.group","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.group(f)\n\nEvaluate f() as an NCCL group: operations issued by f are only submitted when it returns, which makes it possible to issue a collective on several communicators from a single task, or to combine multiple point-to-point operations without deadlocking.\n\nGroups can be nested; operations are submitted when the outermost group ends.\n\nBecause operations in a group are only enqueued on their streams when the outermost group ends, the buffers passed to them must not be read, written, freed, or handed to another task or stream until then. NCCL.jl keeps these buffers, as well as the streams and communicators of the operations, alive until the group ends, so they do not need to be referenced elsewhere. Once the group has ended, the operations are enqueued on their streams, and are ordered like any other work on those streams: the usual CUDA.jl rules for using arrays from other tasks and streams apply.\n\nNCCL keeps track of groups per OS thread, so the following rules apply:\n\nThe current task is pinned to its thread, and stays pinned after the group ends (the same happens when a task uses @async; tasks created by f may still be running afterwards and rely on it). All operations of the group must be issued from this task; operations issued from other tasks, e.g. Threads.@spawn, are not part of the group.\nf must not yield to another task that uses NCCL on the same thread. If that happens, the other task's NCCL calls throw a ConcurrencyViolationError rather than silently joining the group.\n\nIf f throws, the group is still ended, and the exception thrown by f is rethrown, even if ending the group fails as well. What happens to the operations issued before the exception depends on whether NCCL recorded an error in its group state:\n\nNCCL records the errors of operations issued in the group, e.g. when it rejects their arguments. Then the outermost group end discards all queued operations, and submits nothing.\nOther exceptions don't affect the group, and ending it submits the operations that were issued. This includes Julia exceptions, e.g. from the argument checks of NCCL.jl, which run before NCCL is called, and NCCLErrors from NCCL calls that don't record their errors in the group, e.g. when creating an NCCL.UniqueID. If peers wait for operations that were never issued, this may deadlock.\n\nCommunicators are blocking, so ending the group can block, e.g. until the peers have issued their matching operations, before the exception reaches any handler in the caller. Recovering from such a failure, e.g. by having all ranks call NCCL.abort, therefore has to be arranged out of band for the whole job, and can't rely on the failing task. Aborting a blocking communicator is not guaranteed to interrupt a call that is blocked inside NCCL, so terminating the job is the only reliable way out of a hang.\n\nExamples\n\nNCCL.group() do\n    for (comm, sendbuf, recvbuf) in zip(comms, sendbufs, recvbufs)\n        NCCL.Allreduce!(sendbuf, recvbuf, +, comm)\n    end\nend\n\nExternal links\n\nncclGroupStart\nncclGroupEnd\nGroup calls\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.group"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Allreduce!","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Allreduce!(sendbuf, recvbuf, op, comm::Communicator;\n                stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\nNCCL.Allreduce!(sendrecvbuf, op, comm::Communicator; stream)\n\nReduce sendbuf over all ranks using op (one of +, *, min, max, NCCL.avg, or an NCCL.PreMulSum), and write the result to recvbuf on all ranks. Returns recvbuf. Complex numbers can only be reduced with +, NCCL.avg and NCCL.PreMulSum, and Bools with max (logical or) and min (logical and).\n\nsendbuf and recvbuf must have the same element type and length. With a single buffer sendrecvbuf, the operation is performed in place.\n\nThe buffers must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffers must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclAllReduce\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Allreduce!"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Broadcast!","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Broadcast!(sendbuf, recvbuf, comm::Communicator; root::Integer = 0,\n                stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\nNCCL.Broadcast!(sendrecvbuf, comm::Communicator; root, stream)\n\nCopy sendbuf on rank root to recvbuf on all ranks. Returns recvbuf.\n\nsendbuf and recvbuf must have the same element type and length. sendbuf is only read on the root: other ranks can pass nothing instead. With a single buffer sendrecvbuf, the operation is performed in place.\n\nThe buffers must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffers must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclBroadcast\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Broadcast!"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Reduce!","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Reduce!(sendbuf, recvbuf, op, comm::Communicator; root::Integer = 0,\n             stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\nNCCL.Reduce!(sendrecvbuf, op, comm::Communicator; root, stream)\n\nReduce sendbuf over all ranks using op (one of +, *, min, max, NCCL.avg, or an NCCL.PreMulSum), and write the result to recvbuf on rank root. Returns recvbuf. Complex numbers can only be reduced with +, NCCL.avg and NCCL.PreMulSum, and Bools with max (logical or) and min (logical and).\n\nsendbuf and recvbuf must have the same element type and length. recvbuf is only written on the root: other ranks can pass nothing instead. With a single buffer sendrecvbuf, the operation is performed in place.\n\nThe buffers must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffers must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclReduce\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Reduce!"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Allgather!","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Allgather!(sendbuf, recvbuf, comm::Communicator;\n                stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\nNCCL.Allgather!(recvbuf, comm::Communicator; stream)\n\nConcatenate sendbuf from each rank, in rank order, into recvbuf on all ranks. Returns recvbuf.\n\nsendbuf and recvbuf must have the same element type, and recvbuf must have nranks(comm) times as many elements as sendbuf. The operation is performed in place when sendbuf is the part of recvbuf that receives the data of this rank. With only recvbuf, that part is sent: recvbuf then consists of nranks(comm) blocks of equal length, and on rank i (counting from 0) the i-th block holds the data to send.\n\nThe buffers must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffers must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclAllGather\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Allgather!"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.ReduceScatter!","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.ReduceScatter!(sendbuf, recvbuf, op, comm::Communicator;\n                    stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\nNCCL.ReduceScatter!(sendrecvbuf, op, comm::Communicator; stream) -> view\n\nReduce sendbuf over all ranks using op (one of +, *, min, max, NCCL.avg, or an NCCL.PreMulSum), and scatter the result over the ranks: recvbuf on rank i receives the i-th block of the result (counting from 0). Returns recvbuf. Complex numbers can only be reduced with +, NCCL.avg and NCCL.PreMulSum, and Bools with max (logical or) and min (logical and).\n\nsendbuf and recvbuf must have the same element type, and sendbuf must have nranks(comm) times as many elements as recvbuf. The operation is performed in place when recvbuf is the block of sendbuf for this rank. With a single buffer sendrecvbuf, which consists of nranks(comm) blocks of equal length, the result for this rank is written to its own block, and a view of that block is returned; the other blocks are left in an undefined state.\n\nThe buffers must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffers must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclReduceScatter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.ReduceScatter!"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Alltoall!","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Alltoall!(sendbuf, recvbuf, comm::Communicator;\n               stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\n\nExchange blocks of data between all ranks: sendbuf and recvbuf are divided into nranks(comm) blocks of equal length, and the j-th block of sendbuf on rank i is copied to the i-th block of recvbuf on rank j (counting from 0). Returns recvbuf.\n\nsendbuf and recvbuf must have the same element type and length, which must be a multiple of nranks(comm). The operation cannot be performed in place: the buffers must not overlap.\n\nThe buffers must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffers must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclAlltoAll\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Alltoall!"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Gather!","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Gather!(sendbuf, recvbuf, comm::Communicator; root::Integer = 0,\n             stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\n\nConcatenate sendbuf from each rank, in rank order, into recvbuf on rank root. Returns recvbuf.\n\nsendbuf and recvbuf must have the same element type, and recvbuf must have nranks(comm) times as many elements as sendbuf. recvbuf is only written on the root: other ranks can pass nothing instead. The operation is performed in place when sendbuf is the part of recvbuf that receives the data of the root, e.g. view(recvbuf, root*n+1:(root+1)*n) for buffers of n elements.\n\nThe buffers must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffers must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclGather\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Gather!"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Scatter!","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Scatter!(sendbuf, recvbuf, comm::Communicator; root::Integer = 0,\n              stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\n\nDivide sendbuf on rank root into nranks(comm) blocks of equal length, and copy the i-th block to recvbuf on rank i (counting from 0). Returns recvbuf.\n\nsendbuf and recvbuf must have the same element type, and sendbuf must have nranks(comm) times as many elements as recvbuf. sendbuf is only read on the root: other ranks can pass nothing instead. The operation is performed in place when recvbuf is the block of sendbuf for the root, e.g. view(sendbuf, root*n+1:(root+1)*n) for blocks of n elements.\n\nThe buffers must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffers must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclScatter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Scatter!"},{"category":"constant","location":"#NCCL.avg","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.avg\n\nPerform an average operation, i.e. a sum across all ranks, divided by the number of ranks. For integers, the sum wraps around on overflow, and the division truncates towards zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.avg"},{"category":"type","location":"#NCCL.PreMulSum","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.PreMulSum(scalar::Real)\n\nReduction operator that multiplies the values of every rank by scalar before summing them: reducing x over the ranks results in scalar*x₀ + scalar*x₁ + .... It can be used with every reduction (Allreduce!, Reduce! and ReduceScatter!), e.g. NCCL.Allreduce!(buf, NCCL.PreMulSum(0.5f0), comm).\n\nThe multiplication happens in the element type of the buffers: scalar is converted to it with convert when the operation is issued. For floating-point buffers, it is rounded to the nearest representable value. For integer buffers, it has to be representable exactly (e.g. NCCL.PreMulSum(0.5) throws an InexactError with an Int32 buffer), and the products and sums wrap around on overflow, as Julia's integer arithmetic does. Complex buffers are scaled by a real scalar, which is converted to the type of their real and imaginary parts. Bool buffers cannot be reduced with PreMulSum.\n\nOnly real scalars are supported: NCCL multiplies the real and imaginary parts of complex numbers separately, so it cannot multiply them by a complex number.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclRedOpCreatePreMulSum\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.PreMulSum"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Send","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Send(sendbuf, comm::Communicator; dest::Integer,\n          stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\n\nSend sendbuf to rank dest, which must call NCCL.Recv! with a buffer of the same element type and length.\n\nThe buffer must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffer must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclSend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Send"},{"category":"function","location":"#NCCL.Recv!","page":"NCCL.jl","text":"NCCL.Recv!(recvbuf, comm::Communicator; source::Integer,\n           stream::Union{CuStream,Nothing} = nothing)\n\nReceive the data that rank source sends with NCCL.Send into recvbuf. Returns recvbuf.\n\nThe buffer must be stored contiguously in memory of the device of comm: a CuArray, or a contiguous view or reshape of one. The operation is enqueued on stream, which defaults to the task's stream on that device, and is ordered like other work on that stream. Inside an NCCL.group, it is only enqueued when the group ends, and its buffer must not be used until then.\n\nExternal links\n\nncclRecv\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NCCL.Recv!"}]
}
